HST Office: documents saved before the East Asian default font changed, or made from the earlier
HST templates, still name a CJK font (Noto Serif CJK SC, NSimSun, DejaVu Sans ...) as their East
Asian font. Word then stores every precomposed Vietnamese letter (U+1E00-U+1EFF) in a run of its
own when it saves the .docx, and the file opens several times slower afterwards
(hst-writer-cjk-default-font.patch has the measurements).

When the document has no Han, Kana or Hangul character in any text node (body, headers and
footers, footnotes and endnotes, frames), the DOCX export now writes the East Asian font
(w:rFonts/@w:eastAsia in document defaults, styles and runs) equal to the Latin font of the same
style or run, and does not write the stored East Asian font. Documents with East Asian text keep
their East Asian fonts unchanged. The document model is not changed; only the exported file.
The .doc export has its own attribute output and is unchanged.

Test: tests/runtime/check-docx-word-interop.py (Vietnamese document with a CJK East Asian font,
and the same document with Chinese text).
---
--- a/sw/source/filter/ww8/docxattributeoutput.cxx
+++ b/sw/source/filter/ww8/docxattributeoutput.cxx
@@ -8445,6 +8445,11 @@
     AddToAttrList( m_pFontsAttrList,
         FSNS( XML_w, XML_ascii ), sFontName,
         FSNS( XML_w, XML_hAnsi ), sFontName );
+
+    // HST Office: without Han, Kana or Hangul text the East Asian font is the Latin one (see
+    // DocxExport::HasEastAsianText()); CharFontCJK() writes nothing then.
+    if (!GetExport().HasEastAsianText() && !m_pFontsAttrList->hasAttribute(FSNS(XML_w, XML_eastAsia)))
+        AddToAttrList(m_pFontsAttrList, FSNS(XML_w, XML_eastAsia), sFontName);
 }
 
